      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Aim",
          "content": "To describe the abnormalities at MR imaging and related complaints in patients with poor outcome after STARR procedure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Materials and Methods",
          "content": "The medical records of 21 symptomatic patients from centre 1, 31 patients from centre 2, and 63 patients from centre 3 were reviewed with regard to findings at MR defecography and related symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "Regardless of the centre, most relevant imaging features and related complaints were (a) impaired emptying (82.11%), related complaint ODS; (b) persistent rectocele >2\u2009cm and intussusception (39.3%), split evacuation and digitation; (c) pelvic organ descent on straining (39.8%), prolapse sensation; (d) small neorectum and loss of contrast (32.5%), urgency and incontinence; (e) anastomotic stricture and granuloma (28.4%), pain; and (f) nonrelaxing puborectalis muscle (19.5%), tenesmus. Less frequent findings included rectal pocket formation (5.6%) and rectovaginal sinus tract (1.6%). Patients were referred to MR imaging with an average time interval of 5\u2009±\u20092, 4\u2009±\u20091, and 2\u2009±\u20091 years in the three centres, respectively, and only rarely by the same surgeon who performed the operation: 1/21 (4.8%) in centre 1, 3/39 (7.7%) in centre 2, and 9/63 (14.3%) in centre 3."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusion",
          "content": "Most surgeons involved in STARR operation with subsequent poor outcome do not rely on MR imaging."
        }
      ]},
